  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The state of Tennessee has $10.5B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. Federal buyers awarded 17,593 contracts, with an average award size of $597K. Obligations are down 4.1% year over year. The Department of Energy is the dominant buyer with $6.9B in obligations, and Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligations at $5.8B. A small group of vendors capture a large share of obligations: CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC holds $3.1B and UT-BATTELLE LLC holds $2.6B. Obligations by fiscal year show a relatively stable range from $10.8B to $11.4B between FY21 and FY25, with the FY26 to date total at $10.5B. The top buying agencies beyond the Department of Energy include the Department of Defense at $1.4B and the Department of Justice at $709M. Contracting activity centers on environmental remediation, engineering, security, construction, and specialized manufacturing industries, reflecting large programmatic work and major site operations in the state.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed year over year in Tennessee federal contracting?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 4.1% year over year. The FY2026 to date total is $10.5B, down from recent fiscal years that ranged from $10.8B to $11.4B. Award volume remains substantial at 17,593 awards, with the average award size at $597K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors dominate Tennessee federal contracting?",
        "body": "Facilities Support Services is the largest industry with $5.8B in obligations. CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC and UT-BATTELLE LLC are the largest vendors with $3.1B and $2.6B in obligations respectively. Remediation Services, Engineering Services, and Security Guards and Patrol Services follow as significant industry categories with $719M, $687M, and $610M in obligations."
      }
    ]
